// §14 entry 1 -- Turkish dotless-i case fold in program logic, attempted
// without holding `sermo` -> plica_sermone without sermo.
//
// §5.1: `.plica_unicode()` is total and locale-free (Unicode default case
// fold: "I" -> "i", always, no capability). `.plica_sermone(sermo)` is the
// LOCALE-SENSITIVE fold -- under tr-TR, "I" -> dotless "i" (U+0131) -- and
// the capability itself is the argument: a function with no `sermo` in
// scope has no value to pass. CVE-2025-49003 (§2.1) is exactly a validation
// step missing this distinction.
//
// NO CODE EXISTS FOR THIS -- confirmed directly against §2.4's
// unrepresentability table, not just asserted: "Locale case fold in
// program logic | plica_unicode total and locale-free; plica_sermone
// requires sermo | --". The Error column is an em dash, the same as two
// other rows in that table (decimal-separator drift, build nondeterminism)
// and unlike every row around it (which name EXS-E0332, E0103, E0105, ...).
// §14 entry 1 itself points at "plica_sermone without sermo", not at an
// EXS-Exxxx. CLAUDE.md: "Never invent a code" -- so this fixture is graded
// on the ABSENCE of a registered code, not on a code this file must not
// invent.
//
// Mechanically this would be caught the same way EXS-E0421 catches any
// other undeclared-capability use (§4.1: "A function with no `poscit` and
// no capability parameters is pure with respect to ambient state") --
// `normaliza` below declares no `poscit` at all and still names `sermo` in
// its body -- but the spec never spends a registry entry on this specific
// row, so whether a future checker reuses E0421's machinery or something
// new is an open implementation question, not something this fixture can
// settle by construction.
//
// RUNS, as of 2026-09-27, through the runner's `nocap` shape -- which is a
// GRADING RULE and not a new checker: nothing in the compiler changed for it.
// The rule is three conditions, and the third is what makes this entry's
// subject the capability's absence rather than EXS-E0421 in general:
//
//   1. exsc exits 1 (diagnostics were emitted).
//   2. the SET of EXS-E codes is exactly {EXS-E0421} -- a set comparison, so
//      a second, unrelated diagnostic fails it. The directive names that code
//      in `expect-code=` as the code the GENERAL undeclared-capability rule
//      (§4.1) already draws; it is not a code minted for §2.4's row, which
//      has none and must not be given one (CLAUDE.md).
//   3. some diagnostic's machine-readable `fix` offers `poscit sermo`, from
//      the directive's `atom=sermo`. The fix OBJECT's bytes are matched, not
//      the whole JSON line: `message` and `snippet` also name the atom, and
//      matching those would pass a refusal that named the capability without
//      offering the edit. This is the machine-readable statement that the
//      program has no value of this capability to pass and that declaring it
//      is the edit -- which is the entry, in a field a tool reads.
//
// The second half is closed. §5.1's `textus` methods had no declaration site
// (`docs/design/checker.md` finding 14), so running this fixture drew a
// spurious `EXS-E0305` (unknown method) on top of -- and ahead of -- the
// capability-absence this entry is actually about. `plica_sermone` now has a
// declaration: `checker/types/prim.inc`'s prelude row, looked up by the
// receiver's type KIND, with `sermo` as parameter 1 rather than a row,
// because §5.1's own code block passes the capability as a value and this
// entry's whole subject is that a function with no `sermo` in scope has no
// value to pass.
//
// Measured with a fresh `exsc` from the working tree, `--diagnostica json`,
// against THIS file: ONE diagnostic, `EXS-E0421` at `sermo` in
// `s.plica_sermone(sermo)`, message "undeclared capability (atom or row)",
// with `"fix":{"kind":"insert",...,"text":" poscit sermo"}` attached. That is
// the mechanism this header predicted -- "caught the same way EXS-E0421
// catches any other undeclared-capability use" -- and since 2026-09-27 the
// suite re-measures it on every run instead of this comment remembering it.
//
// (§10.1 spelled this function `plica_sermone(t: &textus) -> textus poscit
// sermo` -- one parameter, authority in the row -- while §5.1 and this entry
// spell it with the capability as an argument. The spec held both until
// 884b662 reconciled it in §5.1's favour, because this fixture is what runs
// against the checker's table. Not an `[OPEN]` any more.)

publica functio normaliza(s: textus) -> textus {
    redde s.plica_sermone(sermo);
}

// TEST: entry=1 shape=nocap expect-code=EXS-E0421 atom=sermo status=run
